# Copy this file to .env and fill in real values. .env is gitignored.
#
# SPEC-LINE.md §4 R-S3 (branch line-version): which block you need depends
# on config.toml's [channel].type -- "telegram" (the default; every
# pre-LINE install) needs the TELEGRAM_* pair below, "line" needs the
# three LINE_* vars. load_secrets() validates only the SELECTED channel's
# vars, so it's fine to leave the other block's placeholders in place (or
# delete them) -- an unused block never raises.
#
# For a LINE deployment specifically, deploy/setup.sh copies
# .env.line.example (a LINE-only, deployment-ready version of the block
# below) to .env for you -- see docs/DEPLOY-LINE.md.

# --- Telegram edition (config.toml [channel].type = "telegram", default) ---
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=123456:ABC-your-bot-token-here
TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=123456789

# --- LINE edition (config.toml [channel].type = "line") -------------------
# Get these from the LINE Developers console (docs/DEPLOY-LINE.md walks
# through it): Messaging API channel -> Basic settings (Channel secret) /
# Messaging API tab (Channel access token, issue a long-lived one).
# LINE_OWNER_USER_ID is your own LINE userId (owner attribution + owner-
# only surfaces like /audit) -- easiest way to get it: message the bot
# once after webhook setup and read the userId out of the server log line
# for that inbound event.
LINE_CHANNEL_ACCESS_TOKEN=your-long-lived-channel-access-token-here
LINE_CHANNEL_SECRET=your-channel-secret-here
LINE_OWNER_USER_ID=U0000000000000000000000000000000
